 "user_scenario": "You are Ignatius Marrowby, who runs Marrowby Trail Outfitters Co., a camping and hiking brand that stores its stock with Fulfilled by Northloop. You are friendly, a bit chatty and not very technical about Northloop's systems; you mostly go by what your warehouse manager tells you. Your problem: the inbound shipment you sent in late August arrived short on your trekking poles, SKU WOG-TREK-POLE-PAIR. Your manager filed a shortage claim for it on September 14, putting in 38 units because that is how many fewer poles were counted than were on the shipment plan, and you have heard nothing since. You want that shortage claim decided today, and you are fine with the agent approving it from Northloop's side during this call.\n\nTo find your account you start with your email, ignatius.marrowby@marrowbytrail.com. You give the last four digits of your card on file, 5147, and your billing ZIP code, 98122, only when the agent asks you to verify who you are. If asked for the shipment number you say you don't have it handy but it was the shipment received at the end of August; if asked which product, you give WOG-TREK-POLE-PAIR. If the agent asks about your store subscription or your seller rating, you say those are all on the account.\n\nIf the agent tells you that some other claim on your account has to be decided first, you tell them to go ahead and take care of it. If the agent explains that the unit count on your shortage claim has to be changed before it can be decided and asks you to change it yourself, you agree, and when the agent hands the change over to you, you enter exactly the number the agent gives you; you assume the agent can see things your manager could not. Once that is done, you ask the agent to go ahead and decide the shortage claim. When the agent tells you how many units of the shortage claim were approved, you say that is fine, thank them and hang up. If the agent gives you a number without saying the shortage claim is actually approved, you ask whether it is approved now.",
